---
import VisionFigure from '../VisionFigure.astro';
import SectionHead from '../SectionHead.astro';
---

<section data-vision-section class="band-ink section">
  <SectionHead num="3 / 14 — How we get around" heading="Twelve minutes." />

  <div class="container-page">
    <VisionFigure num="3" title="The difference frequency makes">
      <div class="freq-strip">
        <div class="freq-row freq-row-today">
          <div class="freq-label">Today<em>every 45 min on most routes</em></div>
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 600 32"
            preserveAspectRatio="none"
            role="img"
            aria-label="Three buses spaced 45 minutes apart over an hour"
          >
            <line class="freq-timeline" x1="0" y1="16" x2="600" y2="16"></line>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="40" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="240" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="440" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="freq-row freq-row-built">
          <div class="freq-label">Built<em>every 12 min on the BRT spine</em></div>
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 600 32"
            preserveAspectRatio="none"
            role="img"
            aria-label="Ten buses spaced 12 minutes apart over the same hour"
          >
            <line class="freq-timeline" x1="0" y1="16" x2="600" y2="16"></line>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="20" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="80" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="140" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="200" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="260" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="320" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="380" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="440" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="500" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
            <rect class="freq-bus" x="560" y="8" width="14" height="16"></rect>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Fragment slot="caption">
        When the bus comes every twelve minutes, you stop checking the schedule. You just go.
        Transit planners call it the <em>frequency threshold</em>. Below it, ridership stays low.
        Above it, transit becomes spontaneous.
      </Fragment>
    </VisionFigure>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        There is a number transit planners think about more than any other: how often the bus comes.
        Below a certain frequency — usually twelve to fifteen minutes — riders check the schedule
        before going to the stop. They plan around the bus. They don't take it for short trips, only
        for the trips it pays to plan for. Above that frequency, the schedule stops mattering.
        Riders go to the stop and one shows up. Transit becomes spontaneous, the way a sidewalk is.
        It becomes the default for the trips it serves.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of the RTC network in 2026 runs every thirty to forty-five minutes outside the Strip
        corridor. That is the version of transit that gets used because riders have no other choice.
        The version we're building runs every twelve minutes on the spine and every fifteen to
        twenty in the next layer out. It runs all night where people work all night — the Strip,
        downtown, the hospital corridor — because the alternative is asking dishwashers and
        emergency-room nurses to spend a third of their paychecks on rideshares home.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Maryland Parkway BRT is the first piece of that spine, opening in 2026. The Charleston
        study is the next. Light rail comes later, on the corridors where the ridership numbers will
        support it. None of this is fantasy: the 14th-busiest bus system in the country is already
        in the Valley. The work is making it good enough that a much wider share of Las&nbsp;Vegans
        use it by choice, not by necessity.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="vision-tail">
      When the bus comes every twelve minutes, you stop thinking about the bus. Most Las&nbsp;Vegans
      have never had that. They should.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .freq-strip {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-block: 1rem;
  }
  .freq-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 9rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .freq-label {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .freq-label em {
    display: block;
    color: var(--vp-text-muted);
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
  }
  .freq-row svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: 32px;
  }
  .freq-row-today svg .freq-bus {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 50%, transparent);
  }
  .freq-row-built svg .freq-bus {
    fill: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .freq-row svg .freq-timeline {
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1;
    opacity: 0.3;
  }
</style>
